 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "Fish only for «تأتيهم حيتانهم»; no people, trick or punishment drawn. Light for the admonishers' answer. The fate of the silent group is not glossed (only that the forbidders were saved). STAGING v4: c1 (163): the town's shore by day (sea_shore). The people of the town are three faceless men in plain robes (a group that transgressed: no woman is singled out). A fish plate over the sea for the fish that came to them openly on the Sabbath (163:13-17, both tafsirs: many, floating on the surface); the plate leaves on 163:18 (on other days they did not come). They see them (point toward the sea, 163:14); their trick, the digging of pits, the nets and any catch are never drawn; the fish actor (a fish in a basket) is not used, as it would show a catch. A two-paths plate for the trial (163:24); they turn away on 163:27 (their disobedience). The Prophet, peace be upon him, addressed in 163:1 is not implied: wide shot, no lone man. c2 (164-165): the town by day. Three groups (As-Sa'di): those who warned (three believers, left), those who said why do you warn (three men in plain robes, centre) and the transgressors (an uncountable crowd in dark robes, right: As-Sa'di, most of them). The middle group speaks and points at the transgressors (164:2, 164:7); the camera on the transgressors for Allah will destroy them (164:9); the warners answer (164:14), with a seal plate for an excuse before your Lord (164:15). The middle group walks off at the end of the dialogue (164:19), before any punishment: their fate is disputed (As-Sa'di) and is neither shown saved nor punished. The transgressors turn away (165:2) and leave (165:4); the warners leave on 165:7 with a footsteps plate as they are saved (165:6). The punishment falls only on the empty town: it darkens on 165:11 (by day, not dawn); nothing else of the punishment is drawn (the transformation is in 166, not drawn)."
 },
